#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176655} :activity/id #uuid "275b0afa-011e-599e-83db-08f12becae8b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049126 :topic/id #uuid "dcb0f682-2f5d-57d5-ac9a-10eb5e8a8b36" :topic/math-academy-id 3591} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176950 :tutorial/content "Given two finite data sets of equal length, say,\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}, \\qquad \ny=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\},\n$$\n\nthe **covariance** between $x$ and $y,$ denoted by $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y),$ is a measure that evaluates the extent to which $x$ and $y$ change together.\n\n- A positive covariance means that if $x$ increases, then $y$ is also likely to increase:\n\n![](images/bb/bbfae3f0fb38bc8652cd26716e4b0b128cf1f2039350267034d97161d7f5dac4.png)\n\n- A negative covariance means that if $x$ increases, then $y$ is likely to decrease:\n\n![](images/fd/fd45053fdc39f070e3438c3b8c61fe01613201595cfe00bd4cc6c6e481bf73ab.png)\n\n- A covariance of zero (or close to zero) indicates that there is no *linear* relationship between $x$ and $y.$\n\n![](images/a5/a58fa4d40d77af23df86b5bd8eaca3119b93bc9ea97cc435eb4e53ba2777b071.png)\n\n**Watch Out!** A covariance of zero does *not* mean that $x$ and $y$ are not related. It simply means the relationship between them is not linear. To illustrate, consider the image below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4bdbf92e691174644dda9b9a84acb1b896c0436afb221501dc10b4635841539.png)\n\nThe (nonlinear) relationship shown above is $y=x^2.$ However, it can be shown that $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = 0$ for this data set.\n\nTo calculate $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y),$ we use the following formula: \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$  and $\\overline{y}$ denote the means of $x$ and $y$ respectively.\n\n**Note**: This formula for the covariance assumes that $x$ and $y$ are drawn from a *population*. There is a slightly different formula for covariance when $x$ and $y$ are sample data. However, we won't worry about the difference for now." :tutorial/id #uuid "da03b3e7-b452-59b9-b81e-5dd75cea02a2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10301 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a9620c91-7f1f-5c90-81f9-15b63580b9b6" :step/math-academy-id 217080 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175936 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175746 :question/id #uuid "ef520918-4e4b-516b-acaa-57a14b5c1405" :question/math-academy-id "e-16134"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2ac0065-9315-547a-a9cf-c6a5abea1aed" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050862}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275215}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance"} :step/id #uuid "7c94f745-4162-5e7d-841a-0a7d3f00e84a" :step/math-academy-id 217081 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175937 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175747 :question/id #uuid "33adfe2b-297e-5c52-8c4f-088d0c2b66e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-16136"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cee807c1-dd86-5b0e-a595-5c5754c47e4c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275210}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance in Context"} :step/id #uuid "48e20fe8-234e-5200-b8a8-908f518d0da4" :step/math-academy-id 217084 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176951 :tutorial/content "Variance measures the spread of a *single* data set $x$ around its mean value $\\overline{x}.$ On the other hand, covariance measures the relationship between *two* data sets $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe (population) covariance of the data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}, \\qquad  \ny=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nis given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y}).\n$$\n\nIf we compare a single data set, say $x$, against itself using our formula for covariance, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,x) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\sigma^2$ denotes the variance of $x.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "42b9ca98-be0b-50e0-9b90-f3e38732fcd0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10305 :tutorial/title "The Connection Between Variance and Covariance"} :step/id #uuid "2661268b-e076-5805-af10-02ffd1c3fc5e" :step/math-academy-id 217087}] :activity/title "Covariance" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176135}]}
